# Changelog

## 2026-09-01 — presenterm step type (planned, then implemented same day)

- Prompted by: wanting a step type that cuts a recording to a full-screen
  terminal slideshow (via [presenterm](https://github.com/mfontanini/presenterm))
  instead of only shell/editor activity, for episodes that want to present
  slides mid-demo.
- Captured via a `grill-me`-style design session; verified presenterm's
  real slide-separator syntax against its own docs before building — it
  uses `<!-- end_slide -->` HTML comments, not a `---` thematic break (which
  is reserved for an optional YAML front matter block that adds its own
  intro slide).
- Added [FR-CLI-013](./functional-requirements/cli-pipeline.md#fr-cli-013--presenterm-step-type)
  (silent content write via an unrecorded `docker exec` subprocess —
  mirroring [FR-CLI-010](./functional-requirements/cli-pipeline.md#fr-cli-010--write_vim-step-diffliveedit-mode)'s
  pattern — plus auto-detected slide count and automatic slide-advance/quit
  keystrokes) and [FR-EDIT-009](./functional-requirements/scenario-authoring.md#fr-edit-009--presenterm-step-schema)
  (the step's schema: `path` + content, one optional `slide_pause` field, no
  diff-mode fields, no typing-time guardrail). Corrected
  [FR-EDIT-001](./functional-requirements/scenario-authoring.md#fr-edit-001--step-schema-validation)'s
  stale "one of three types" wording to "one of five."
- `presenterm` installed into the existing `rust` flavour
  (`flavours/rust/Dockerfile`) from a prebuilt musl release binary rather
  than `cargo install` — sidesteps the base image's older Rust toolchain
  (possible MSRV mismatch) and avoids glibc-version coupling a
  `-unknown-linux-gnu` binary would have against bullseye's glibc.
- Verified: `backend/tests/test_api.py` extended with round-trip,
  required-field, `slide_pause` positivity, and typing-guardrail-exemption
  tests for `presenterm`; `frontend`'s `tsc -b && vite build`/`oxlint` pass.
  Later the same day, verified end-to-end against real `docker`+`presenterm`
  (`python3 driver.py` with a live `presenterm` step), which surfaced and
  fixed two real bugs the offline-only checks above couldn't catch:
  (1) the release tarball extracts its binary into a versioned subdirectory
  (`presenterm-<version>/presenterm`), not at the archive root, so the
  original `tar -C /usr/local/bin presenterm` in `flavours/rust/Dockerfile`
  found nothing by that name and failed the image build — fixed by
  extracting to `/tmp` and `mv`-ing the binary out; (2) presenterm probes
  the terminal for image support and device attributes on startup and
  blocks on stdin waiting for a reply, which a real terminal emulator sends
  automatically but the recorded pty (a raw pexpect pty, no emulator) never
  would — the step hung indefinitely until `run_presenterm` was changed to
  send a synthetic `\x1b[?c` Device Attributes response right after opening
  presenterm (see [FR-CLI-013](./functional-requirements/cli-pipeline.md#fr-cli-013--presenterm-step-type)).
  With both fixes, a full scenario (command → presenterm, 3 slides → command)
  recorded start to finish with no hang and no visible markdown source in
  the `.cast`, matching the design goal.

### Decisions made during the design session (recommended option chosen each time)

- Slide timing: auto-detected slide count plus one uniform `slide_pause`
  field — not a per-slide pause list, not support for presenterm's `<!--
  pause -->` incremental reveals (deferred to a future iteration).
- Step shape: self-contained (write + present in one step, like
  `write_vim`) — no diff/edit mode, decks are always written fresh.
- Content delivery: written into the container **silently** via an
  unrecorded subprocess, not typed visibly like `write_file`'s heredoc —
  the recording should show the slideshow playing, not markdown source
  being typed. Consequence: no typing-time guardrail applies.
- Flavour: installed into the existing `rust` flavour, not a new dedicated
  flavour.
- Binary install method: a prebuilt musl release binary, not `cargo
  install` — avoids both an MSRV risk against the base image's 2022-era
  Rust toolchain and a glibc-version risk a `-gnu` binary would carry.
- Default `slide_pause`: 2.0s.
- Recording-length guardrail (slide_count × slide_pause): explicitly
  deferred as an accepted v1 gap, rather than bundled into this change —
  symmetric with how `write_vim`'s own typing-time guardrail
  ([FR-EDIT-007](./functional-requirements/scenario-authoring.md#fr-edit-007--typing-time-guardrail-on-write_vim-content))
  arrived as its own follow-up rather than day-one.

## 2026-08-31 — Use Cases marketing page

- Prompted by: wanting a second public page that gives a prospective
  visitor a clearer picture of what termreel is for, beyond the mechanics
  pitched on the landing page.
- Added a new domain, `FR-SITE-*` ([Public marketing site](./functional-requirements/public-site.md)),
  since the marketing site had no FRs at all in the initial baseline (it
  wasn't part of the 2026-08-30 code audit's scope). Added
  [FR-SITE-001](./functional-requirements/public-site.md#fr-site-001--landing-page)
  documenting the pre-existing landing page as-is, and a new "Prospective
  visitor" actor in [Actors & context](./actors-and-context.md).
- Added [FR-SITE-002](./functional-requirements/public-site.md#fr-site-002--shared-site-header-and-footer)
  (`frontend/src/components/marketing/SiteHeader.tsx`/`SiteFooter.tsx`,
  extracted from `Landing.tsx`'s previously-inlined header/footer so both
  marketing pages share one nav) and
  [FR-SITE-003](./functional-requirements/public-site.md#fr-site-003--use-cases-page)
  (`frontend/src/pages/UseCases.tsx`, a `/use-cases` route listing use
